<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fazughli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fazogli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fazughli is a region on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a> in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It was a frontier province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj Sultanate of Sennar</a> and an important source of gold, which was traded north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The search for its gold was one motive for the Egyptian conquest of the Sudan in 1820–1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fazughli.jpg" alt="Fazughli" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Edmund Brehm · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fazughli_village,_c._1850.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oral traditions hold that the kingdom lived on in the <b>Fazughli</b> hills until the Funj conquered it in 1685.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Gold from <b>Fazughli</b> and the borderlands with Ethiopia, ivory, civet, ostrich feathers and, above all, enslaved people captured in raids on the peoples of the southern and western frontiers were sent north to Egypt by caravan or east to the Red Sea port of Suakin, in exchange for…</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
